\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Separator reconstruction on the four-cycle}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{Apply [separator gluing](fdip-0066) to the unit four-cycle with mass #{m=1}.}

\texfig{\begin{tikzpicture}[
  vertex/.style={circle,draw,minimum size=8mm,inner sep=1pt},
  separator/.style={vertex,double,fill=blue!10}
]
\node[separator] (v0) at (0,1.8) {$0$};
\node[vertex] (v1) at (2.4,1.8) {$1$};
\node[separator] (v2) at (2.4,0) {$2$};
\node[vertex] (v3) at (0,0) {$3$};
\draw (v0) -- (v1) -- (v2) -- (v3) -- (v0);
\end{tikzpicture}}

\p{The doubled vertices form the separator #{S=\{0,2\}}.
The cover sets are #{V_1=\{0,1,2\}} and #{V_2=\{0,2,3\}};
their private vertices #{1,3} have no connecting edge. The graph
has only the four drawn edges, with no diagonal edge. Its precision is

##{Q=\begin{pmatrix}
3&-1&0&-1\\-1&3&-1&0\\0&-1&3&-1\\-1&0&-1&3
\end{pmatrix}.}
Use #{V_1=(0,1,2)}, #{V_2=(0,2,3)} and #{S=(0,2)}, with the listed
coordinate orders. Each omitted vertex has precision #{3}, so the
[Schur formula](fdip-000D) gives
##{\begin{aligned}
\Lambda_1&=\begin{pmatrix}
8/3&-1&-1/3\\-1&3&-1\\-1/3&-1&8/3
\end{pmatrix},\\
\Lambda_2&=\begin{pmatrix}
8/3&-1/3&-1\\-1/3&8/3&-1\\-1&-1&3
\end{pmatrix},\\
\Lambda_S&=\begin{pmatrix}7/3&-2/3\\-2/3&7/3\end{pmatrix}.
\end{aligned}}
Both triple laws have this same overlap marginal. Padding and
subtracting gives #{Q} entry by entry: each overlap diagonal is
#{8/3+8/3-7/3=3}, and the induced overlap entry is
#{-1/3-1/3+2/3=0}. The remaining edges retain weight #{-1}.
The determinants are #{15,15,5}, respectively, so the glued
normalization gives #{\det Q=15\cdot15/5=45}, agreeing with its
eigenvalues #{1,3,3,5}. This uses one separator of a [non-chordal](fdip-0068)
cycle; it does not assert that arbitrary edge laws on that cycle glue.}
